ing about how the backend resolves assets and returns a renderable scene. The web can use a quick render for local fixture loading, but I wonder if it should fetch the scene and manifest for a live API. The step highlights server-side asset resolution, so it may be best for the backend to produce the render response through the same mapping. The web could then only use this for fixture mode. I’m a bit confused about the client-side reconstruction aspect from step 6.\"},{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Exploring new package implementation**\\n\\nI'm considering implementing a new package, `packages/render`, which would include types like AssetManifestEntry and ResolvedAssetBinding. There could also be QuickRenderNode and QuickRenderScene types that encompass things like scene_id and asset_bindings. Additionally, I want to ensure the function `resolveSceneAssets(scene, manifest)` provides asset bindings and proxy fallbacks when assets are missing. I think we need a scenario to verify these fallback misses and might need to create tests or scripts to check for manipulated scenes with missing assets.\"},{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Evaluating simplicity in functions**\\n\\nI find this situation acceptable, even if it's somewhat non-standard. It involves pure mapping and types, which is interesting. Since there are no constraints, I think we could create types along with pure functions. Keeping it simple seems to be the best approach here. It feels like it doesn’t need to be overly complicated or extensive, which is nice because simplicity often leads to clarity.\"},{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Clarifying mapping implementation**\\n\\nI'm working on implementing a mapping system. First, I'll resolve each surface to create a render surface with material information, translating floor and wall materials into a material appearance string without needing an asset. \\n\\nNext, I’ll resolve objects by sorting them and determining requested assets from either object references or the editing asset references. If the requested asset is in the manifest, I'll use it; otherwise, I’ll fallback on proxy assets. I’m also considering fixed elements as procedural boxes without assets for synchronization.\"},{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Verifying test responses**\\n\\nI need to ensure that my tests verify the quick render response against the manipulated fixture with an unknown asset fallback. It’s really important that the quick render response maintains the same version as the layout scene read. I’m thinking that maybe I could use a script to assert this condition. It’ll help ensure consistency between different elements of the rendering process, which seems crucial for accuracy overall. I should keep looking into this.\"},{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Exploring render mapping**\\n\\nI'm working on a scene-to-render mapping between the backend and web, but it doesn’t have to rely on browser JavaScript. I can satisfy this by having both the API server and web development server import the same mapping for efficient rendering. The browser would then use this render response in both live and fixture modes. I plan to implement this common mapping in the specified file and ensure version synchronization by displaying matching versions. I’m considering a fixture server route for quick rendering as well!\"}]}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_nxUzFCsYLIeKIuR1M7bQ74cx|fc_0c1af01cdcf317f80169e29046d79c819a864d3dfab34c9241","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"packages/contracts/src/scene.ts","offset":260,"limit":120},"partialJson":"{\"path\":\"packages/contracts/src/scene.ts\",\"offset\":260,\"limit\":120}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6b2fz5tCoidRizQDdTAZxpm7|fc_0c1af01cdcf317f80169e29046d7c0819aae54dcc3bee16b91","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"packages/contracts/src/index.ts"},"partialJson":"{\"path\":\"packages/contracts/src/index.ts\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_XVUSQxb8EnfbU11YwaBDHGpQ|fc_0c1af0